WHO CAN DO WHAT:
There are three tiers of people, and a module's file ending says which
tier may use it.

owner   .omod   Anyone on files/lists/owners.lst, in any channel. System
                level: loading and unloading modules, the owner list,
                sending the bot into other channels.
admin   .amod   Per channel: that channel's broadcaster, its moderators,
                and anyone on its admin list. Admins cannot touch the
                module system or any other channel.
anyone  .umod   Everybody in chat.

THE BUILT-IN COMMANDS:
(shown with the default trigger, which is !)

Owners:
!modules                      list active and inactive modules
!load <module>                move a module from inactive to active
!unload <module>              and back again
!o2u !u2o !o2a !a2o !u2a !a2u <module>
                              change a module's tier. The letter after
                              the 2 is the tier it moves to.
!owner list | add <name> | rm <name>

Admins:
!admin list                   this channel's admins
!admin add <name> | rm <name> the broadcaster or an owner only

Loading and unloading take effect at once. The bot does not restart.

THE MODULES:
additem.omod    !additem <list> <item>   add a line to a list in files/lists
ascii.omod      !ascii <prefix>          change what the bot puts before its replies
date.umod       !date
fortune.umod    !fortune [category]
join.omod       !join <channel>          send the bot into another channel
part.amod       !part [channel]          a broadcaster sends the bot away from
                                         their own channel; owners can name any
pig2.umod       !pig                     the dice game. !pig alone lists its commands
ping.omod       !ping <host>
rolldice.umod   !rolldice [dice sides throws]
smack.umod      !smack <target>
sniglet.omod    !sniglet_on, !sniglet_off
sniglet.umod    !sniglet [number]        needs files/lists/sniglets.lst, which
                                         you supply: one per line, as number|text
                                         (see sniglets.lst.example)
time.umod       !time
translate.umod  !translate <language> <word>
trig.omod       !trig <new trigger>      always answers to !trig
trig.umod       ?trig                    always answers to ?trig
uptime.umod     !botuptime               how long the bot's machine has been up

A module is a piece of bash that looks at ${whatSaid} and answers with
sendMessage or sendAction. Read one of the small ones (time.umod, date.umod)
and you know how to write your own. Put new modules in bin/inactivemodules
and load them.

THE CONSOLE:
bin/bbots-ctl talks to the running bot from the machine it runs on, without
anything appearing in chat.

bin/bbots-ctl modules              one command; the reply comes back to you
bin/bbots-ctl                      a prompt, for several in a row
bin/bbots-ctl -l smack crouse      -l is loud: the reply goes to the channel
bin/bbots-ctl -c '#channel' ...    act in another channel the bot is in

At the prompt: +<command> sends one command loud, /loud and /quiet switch
for everything after, /channel <name> changes channel, /name <name> changes
the name console commands run under (and saves it), /help lists these.

Console commands run as an owner, under the name in CONSOLE_NAME
(bot-handler unless you change it).

THE TWITCH LOGIN:
The bot logs in with its own token, kept in files/.token and renewed by
the bot itself each time it connects.

bin/gettoken.sh check      whose token is saved, and how long it lasts
bin/gettoken.sh login      log the bot account in again

If the bot has been off for more than about 30 days, it will say it has no
usable token. Run login again, or use option 12 in runme.sh.

HISTORY:
This project is really the brainchild of Joshua Bailey. (aka jbsnake). He was looking for something fun to code in bash, (2005) and decided that there weren't any bash bots coded that interested him much. He did find some code from jasonwoof.orgv (see below), but it wasn't complete, and was basically just the foundation for what was to become bashbots. I doubt there is even a line of the original code in the bashbot.sh file..... but there might be one or two. It was the original idea that we would like to give Jason credit for. The implementation is all us baby !!!!!  :)

jbsnake had been messing around with creating a bash bot for awhile, mainly re-writing and creating a new bashbot.sh and startme.sh files..... and he invited me to join him and play (still 2005). (I'm crouse btw). I joined and started writing the runme.sh file and working on mods here and there. We worked on building a base bashbot and some modules for all of 2006. Sometimes months would go by with neither of us doing much to bbots. In 2005 we applied to sf.net for the bbots name and I registered bbots.org, knowing SOME day we would actually release some code :)

In early 2007, we asked Nukes (aka Richard Whitty) to code an opensource tcpclient in c, and he did. He also agreed to gpl it so we could release bbots with an included binary (and source code) to make running the bots a simpler process. The first release of bbots "beta .10" was in early 2007.

THE TWITCH PORT (2026):
In October 2026 jbsnake found a copy of BETA 2.0 from June 2007 on a backup drive and moved it to Twitch chat. It is still bash, modules still load and unload without a restart, and most of the original modules came along. The port was written with the help of an AI assistant (Claude).

What changed, and why:
- ntcpclient is retired. Twitch requires an encrypted connection, so ncat holds it. Thanks again Nukes: the design ntcpclient made possible is the one still in use.
- NickServ and ChanServ are gone. The bot logs in with a token from Twitch.
- The bot can sit in many channels. The join and part modules manage that.
- A third tier, admin, sits between owner and anyone, so a channel's own broadcaster and moderators have some say in their channel without reaching the module system.
- The trigger is ! (it was .) because Twitch keeps . and / for itself.
- Twitch bots cannot send private messages over chat, so anything that used to be a private message is said in the channel.
- Modules check what they are given. In 2007 the bot sat in a channel of friends. Twitch chat is public.
- pig2's commands all live under !pig, and each channel has its own game. The 2007 win records carry over.
- translate asks the MyMemory service, since the page it used to read is gone.
- uptime answers to !botuptime, because !uptime means something else on Twitch.

What was left behind:
- irccommands (op, deop, kick). This is an entertainment bot, not a moderator.
- beep and beep_toggle.
- cal. Eight lines of calendar is a lot for Twitch chat.
The originals of all of these are in the 2007 release.

THE ORIGINAL IDEA:
http://jasonwoof.org/4th/__images/__images/jason_s_old_programming_projects
QUOTE:  "Here are some of my former programming projects. I don't work on these anymore, or have any intention to work on them in the future. But they are all still available under the terms of the GNU GPL so anyone is free to take over where I left off."

So we did....... you can see some of his original code in the bashbot.sh file (not much of his code really remains, but it was what we started with).  Most of it was re-written and changed.  Thanks Jason for providing the code under the GPL, we appreciate it.  :)  We have greatly modified and expanded the original code and it really doesn't even resemble what Jason started with anymore, but we felt he should be acknowledged and wanted to thank him.

THE CODE TREE:

The basic directory structure is:
bbots-twitch
      runme.sh                the menu
      /bin
      |   startbot.sh         opens the connection, keeps the bot running
      |   bashbot.sh          the bot's main loop
      |   bbots-ctl           the console
      |   gettoken.sh         the Twitch login
      |   /modules            active modules
      |   /inactivemodules    modules waiting to be loaded
      |   /libs               .functions, .core (owner commands),
      |                       .admincore (admin commands)
      |
      /files
      |   config
      |   License, changelog
      |   /lists              owners.lst, channels.lst, smack.lst, sniglets.lst
      |   |   /admins         one admin list per channel
      |   /pig2               win records, and one folder per channel
      |
      /logs                   botlog, one chat log per channel, startbot.out
      /run                    the console's pipe and the bot's pid

